//BUG="v[i] = v[j] is rejected as non-assignable"
//OUT="2 2"

// Not in FEEDBACK.md. Vec's operator[] returns &T. Writing a plain value works
// (`v[0] = 7`), and so does writing an expression (`v[0] = v[1] + 0`), but
// assigning one element straight to another is rejected with
// "Left hand side is non-assignable" — the reference on the right is not
// dereferenced, and check_refs then refuses the whole assignment.
//
// That makes the ordinary three-line swap
//     tmp = v[0]
//     v[0] = v[1]
//     v[1] = tmp
// impossible to write, which for a competitive-programming language is a
// sharp edge: sorting and partitioning are exactly this. The workaround used
// in 12_stdlib/22_a_whole_small_program.cpl is to add `+ 0` to force a value.

fn main
    v = Vec()
    v.push(1)
    v.push(2)
    v[0] = v[1]
    out "{v[0]} {v[1]}"
